  "response": "The Maine was destroyed on 15 February 1898; in other words, the armored cruiser blew up on the night of February 15, 1898, while lying at anchor in Havana harbor, where she had been sent to safeguard American interests during the Cuban insurrection. More than 250 members of the Maine's crew died in the explosion, which came before Navy divers examined the submerged wreck for the 1898 court of inquiry. Those divers worked in murky water, among twisted plating and wreckage, and their reports supplied the physical record on which the court's reasoning would rest. Every later investigation inherited the same basic difficulty: the decisive evidence lay underwater, damaged, and open to more than one reading.\n\nThe 1898 court of inquiry cited a section of keel bent into an inverted V shape as evidence. That deformation was the most dramatic feature the divers described, because a keel driven upward appeared to require a force acting from beneath the hull. The bent structure, however, admitted of two mechanisms, and at least one of them must have operated, perhaps both: the keel deformation was produced by the force of the explosion, or it was produced as the wreck sank and settled into the harbor mud. A hull weakened by a massive forward blast and then pressed into soft sediment could plausibly fold in ways that mimic an upward thrust, so the shape of the steel alone could not fix the location of the initiating event.\n\nThe court concluded that a submarine mine had set off the forward magazines, and that verdict confirms the evidentiary role the court had assigned to the bent keel. It reached this conclusion although a coal bunker on the Maine adjoined one of its forward reserve magazines, an arrangement that offered an internal ignition path the court treated as secondary. A Spanish commission, by contrast, attributed the explosion to a cause inside the ship, reasoning from the absence of the phenomena normally associated with a mine, such as a column of water or dead fish in the harbor. The dispute remained unsettled until the wreck itself could be seen in the open air. In 1911 engineers built a cofferdam around the wreck to expose the hull for inspection, and that exposure led the 1911 board of inspection to conclude that the explosion was entirely internal. Draining the site allowed investigators to measure plating, trace the direction in which frames had been bent, and photograph structures the divers of 1898 had only touched in darkness.\n\nRickover's study of the Maine was published in 1956; by contrast with the boards that had stood over the drained hull, it worked from the surviving documents, drawings, and photographs, and it concluded that heat from a coal-bunker fire most likely ignited the adjacent magazine. That analysis settles the tension between the 1898 mine verdict and the bunker's position beside the reserve magazine, since spontaneous combustion in bituminous coal was a recognized hazard aboard warships of the period, and a fire smoldering against a shared bulkhead could raise the temperature of stored powder to the point of ignition without any outside agency. The physical question therefore reduces to two accounts that cannot both be true, one of which must hold: either the detonation of the Maine's forward magazines was initiated by an external mine, or, because that bunker fire supplied the heat, the detonation of the Maine's forward magazines was initiated by a source inside the ship. The U.S. Navy subsequently issued a formal determination adopting the internal-source account as its official finding and ruling out an external mine. Each investigation decided between these by weighting the same few features of the wreck differently, chiefly the bent keel, the direction of plate deformation, and the placement of the bunker, so that its verdict reflected how much interpretive weight it was prepared to give to steel that had been torn apart, sunk, and buried in mud.",
